------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\;(0\\le k\\le n)\\).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_k:=\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\quad(\\mathcal D_0:=\\varnothing).\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:=D_{\\,i-1}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{C1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_a:=\\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\,\\}\n        =\\{\\,1-a,2-a,\\dots ,n+1-2a\\,\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\Delta_a\\subseteq\\mathcal D_k\\) we need no further line for the column\n\\(x=a\\); otherwise we add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_a:\\;x=a . \\tag{C2}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_k:=\\{V_a\\mid 1\\le a\\le n,\\;\\Delta_a\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal D_k\\}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Number of vertical lines).\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal V_k|=n-k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor \\(a\\le n-k\\) we have \\(1-a\\le -(n-k-1)<0\\notin\\mathcal D_k\\); hence\n\\(\\Delta_a\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal D_k\\) and \\(V_a\\in\\mathcal V_k\\).  \nFor \\(a>n-k\\) the largest element of \\(\\Delta_a\\) is \\(n+1-2a\\le k-1\\) and\nthe smallest is \\(1-a\\ge -(k-1)\\); consequently every element of\n\\(\\Delta_a\\) lies in \\(\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,k-1\\}\\). Since \\(\\mathcal D_k\\) already\ncontains all non\u2011negative numbers up to \\(k-1\\), the only possible\nelements of \\(\\Delta_a\\) not in \\(\\mathcal D_k\\) are the negative ones.\nBut for \\(a>n-k\\) we have \\(1-a\\ge -(k-1)\\) and the whole interval\n\\(\\Delta_a\\) is contained in \\(\\mathcal D_k\\cup\\{-(k-1),\\dots ,-1\\}\\); the\nnegative part is empty because \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) forces \\(1-a\\ge -(k-1)\\) and\n\\(n+1-2a\\ge 0\\). Hence \\(\\Delta_a\\subseteq\\mathcal D_k\\) and no vertical\nline is added.  \nThus exactly the columns \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) contribute a vertical line,\ngiving \\(|\\mathcal V_k|=n-k\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Covering property).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one line of the set  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k:=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\cup\\mathcal V_k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a\\ge 0\\):*  \nSet \\(d:=b-a\\).  \nIf \\(d\\in\\mathcal D_k\\) then \\((a,b)\\in D_d=L_{d+1}\\).  \nIf \\(d\\notin\\mathcal D_k\\) then, by definition of \\(\\mathcal V_k\\),\n\\(\\Delta_a\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal D_k\\); consequently \\(V_a\\in\\mathcal V_k\\)\nand \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_a\\).\n\n*If \\(b-a<0\\):*  \nWrite \\(c:=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le c\\le n\\)). The point satisfies \\(x+y=c\\), i.e.\n\\((a,b)\\in S_c\\).  \nBecause \\(b-a<0\\) we have \\(a\\le n-k\\) (otherwise Lemma\u202f1 would give\n\\(b-a\\ge 0\\)). Hence \\(\\Delta_a\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal D_k\\) and the vertical\nline \\(V_a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_k\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) also lies\non \\(V_a\\). Thus the point is covered. \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Sunny\u2011line count).\n\nThe configuration \\(\\mathcal L_k\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nAll lines \\(L_i\\) have slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\)\nand \\(-1\\); therefore each \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  \nEvery line in \\(\\mathcal V_k\\) is vertical, i.e. has infinite slope, and\nhence is **not** sunny.  Consequently the number of sunny lines equals\n\\(|\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}|=k\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof.\n\nFrom Lemma\u202f3 we have \\(|\\mathcal L_k|=k+(n-k)=n\\); Lemma\u202f4 shows that the\n\\(n\\) lines cover all points of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\); Lemma\u202f5 shows that exactly\n\\(k\\) of them are sunny.  Thus for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\nthere exists a required configuration.\n\nConversely, any admissible configuration consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nso