\Delta_{a}\\) shows that  \n\n   \\[\n   |\\mathcal V_{k}|=n-k . \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   Indeed, for \\(a\\le n-k\\) the smallest element of \\(\\Delta_{a}\\) is \\(1-a\\le -(n-k-1)<0\\), which is not in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); hence \\(V_{a}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\).  \n   For \\(a>n-k\\) we have \\(1-a\\ge -(k-1)\\) and the largest element \\(n+1-2a\\) is \\(\\le k-1\\); consequently every element of \\(\\Delta_{a}\\) lies in \\(\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and no vertical line is added. Thus exactly the columns \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) contribute a vertical line, giving (6).\n\n   *Total number of lines.*  \n   The family  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L_{k}:=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal V_{k}\n   \\]\n\n   therefore contains  \n\n   \\[\n   k+(n-k)=n\n   \\]\n\n   distinct lines: the \\(k\\) sunny lines have slope \\(1\\) and cannot coincide with any vertical line, and the vertical lines are pairwise distinct because they have different \\(x\\)-coordinates.\n\n4. **Verification that every required point is covered.**  \n\n   Let \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n   *Case 1 \u2013 \\(b-a\\ge 0\\).*  \n   Put \\(d:=b-a\\).  \n   If \\(d\\le k-1\\) then \\(d\\in\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) and \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(D_{d}=L_{d+1}\\).  \n   If \\(d\\ge k\\) then \\(d\\notin\\{0,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\); by definition of \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\) the column \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\), i.e. the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) is present, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_{a}\\).\n\n   *Case 2 \u2013 \\(b-a<0\\).*  \n   Write \\(c:=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le c\\le n\\)). The point satisfies the equation \\(x+y=c\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(S_{c}\\) of slope \\(-1\\).  \n   Because \\(b-a<0\\), from (1) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   -(a-1)\\le b-a\\le -1\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad a\\le n-k .\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   (Indeed, if \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) then the upper bound in (1) would be at least \\(0\\), contradicting \\(b-a<0\\).)  \n   Hence \\(a\\le n-k\\) and, by the discussion in the proof of (6), the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\). Consequently \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(V_{a}\\). Thus every point with \\(b-a<0\\) is covered as well.\n\n   In both cases the point \\((a,b)\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\); therefore the covering condition is satisfied.\n\n5. **Sunny\u2011line count.**  \n   By construction the only sunny lines are the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\); all vertical lines have infinite slope and are not sunny. Hence the configuration contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n6. **Extremal values.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* \\(\\mathcal L_{0}\\) consists of the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(V_{1},\\dots ,V_{n}\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\):* \\(\\mathcal L_{n}\\) consists of the \\(n\\) sunny lines \\(D_{0},D_{1},\\dots ,D_{n-1}\\).  \n   Both satisfy the required properties.\n\n7. **Converseness.**  \n   Any admissible configuration must contain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. The construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is indeed attainable. Hence the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 1 (Bounds for the difference).\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\),\n\n\\[\nb-a\\in\\{-\\,(a-1),\\dots ,\\,n+1-2a\\}.\n\\tag{L1}\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nSince \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\),\n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge 1-a=-(a-1),\\qquad\nb-a\\le n+1-a-a=n+1-2a .\n\\qquad\\blacksquare\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 2 (Two families covering the points).\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\nD_{d}:\\;y=x+d\\;(d\\in\\mathbb Z),\\qquad\nS_{c}:\\;x+y=c\\;(c\\in\\mathbb Z),\\qquad\nV_{a}:\\;x=a\\;(a\\in\\mathbb Z).\n\\]\n\nFor every \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\)\n\n* either \\(b-a\\ge 0\\) and \\((a,b)\\in D_{\\,b-a}\\);\n* or \\(b-a<0\\) and \\((a,b)\\in S_{\\,a+b}\\).\n\n*Proof.* Direct substitution into the equations of the lines. \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Construction for a given \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\)).\n\nSet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal D_{k}:=\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\quad(\\mathcal D_{0}:=\\varnothing).\n\\]\n\nTake the \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}=D_{\\,i-1}\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,k). \\tag{C1}\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(a\\) with \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{a}:=\\{\\,b-a\\mid (a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\}\n          =\\{\\,1-a,2-a,\\dots ,n+1-2a\\,\\}. \\tag{C2}\n\\]\n\nIf \\(\\Delta_{a}\\subseteq\\mathcal D_{k}\\) we add nothing; otherwise we add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nV_{a}:x=a . \\tag{C3}\n\\]\n\nDefine  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal V_{k}:=\\{V_{a}\\mid 1\\le a\\le n,\\;\\Delta_{a}\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal D_{k}\\}. \\tag{C4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 3 (Number of vertical lines).\n\n\\[\n|\\mathcal V_{k}|=n-k .\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nConsider the interval \\(\\Delta_{a}=\\{1-a,\\dots ,n+1-2a\\}\\).\n\n*If \\(a\\le n-k\\):* then \\(1-a\\le -(n-k-1)<0\\). Since \\(\\mathcal D_{k}\\) contains only non\u2011negative integers, the element \\(1-a\\) is not in \\(\\mathcal D_{k}\\); therefore \\(\\Delta_{a}\\not\\subseteq\\mathcal D_{k}\\) and \\(V_{a}\\in\\mathcal V_{k}\\).\n\n*If \\(a>n-k\\):* then \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\). We have  \n\n\\[\n1-a\\ge -(k-1),\\qquad n+1-2a\\le n+1-2(n-k+1)=k-1 .\n\\]\n\nHence every element of \\(\\Delta_{a}\\) lies between \\(-(k-1)\\) and \\(k-1\\). The non\u2011negative part of this interval is \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}=\\mathcal D_{k}\\); the negative part is empty because the lower bound \\(-(k-1)\\) is attained only when \\(a=n-k+1\\), in which case the upper bound \\(n+1-2a\\) equals \\(k-1\\ge 0\\). Consequently \\(\\Delta_{a}\\subseteq\\mathcal D_{k}\\) and no vertical line is added.\n\nThus exactly the columns \\(a=1,\\dots ,n-k\\) contribute a vertical line, giving \\(|\\mathcal V_{k}|=n-k\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 4 (Covering property).\n\nEvery point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one line of  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{k}:=\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}\\cup\\mathcal V_{k}.\n\\]\n\n*Proof.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\).\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(b-a\\ge 0\\).*  \nPut \\(d:=b-a\\).  \n\n- If \\(d\\le k-1\\) then \\(d\\in\\mathcal D_{k}\\) and \\((a,b)\\in D_{d}=L_{d+1}\\).  \n- If \\(d\\ge k\\) then \\(d\\notin\\mathcal D_{k}\\); by definition of \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\) the column \\(x=a\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\), i.e. the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) is present, and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(V_{a}\\).\n\n*Case 2 \u2013 \\(b-a<0\\).*  \nWrite \\(c:=a+b\\) (so \\(2\\le c\\le n\\)). The point satisfies \\(x+y=c\\), i.e. it lies on the line \\(S_{c}\\) of slope \\(-1\\).  \nFrom Lemma\u202f1 we have  \n\n\\[\nb-a\\ge -(a-1)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b-a\\le -1 .\n\\]\n\nThe upper bound \\(-1\\) together with (1) yields  \n\n\\[\n-(a-1)\\le -1\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;a\\le n-k .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n(Indeed, if \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) then the upper bound in (1) would be at least \\(0\\), contradicting \\(b-a<0\\).)  \nThus \\(a\\le n-k\\), and by the analysis in Lemma\u202f3 the vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) belongs to \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\). Hence \\((a,b)\\) also lies on \\(V_{a}\\).\n\nIn both cases the point belongs to a line of \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 5 (Sunny\u2011line count).\n\nThe configuration \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe lines \\(L_{i}=D_{i-1}\\) have slope \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nAll lines in \\(\\mathcal V_{k}\\) are vertical, i.e. have infinite slope, and are not sunny.  \nThus the number of sunny lines equals \\(|\\{L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k}\\}|=k\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Lemma 6 (Distinctness of the lines).\n\nThe set \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) have slope \\(1\\) and are pairwise distinct because their intercepts \\(i-1\\) are different.  \nEach vertical line \\(V_{a}\\) has slope \\(\\infty\\); therefore no vertical line can coincide with any \\(L_{i}\\).  \nDifferent values of \\(a\\) give different equations \\(x=a\\), so the vertical lines are pairwise distinct.  \nConsequently \\(|\\mathcal L_{k}|=k+(n-k)=n\\). \\(\\blacksquare\\)\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### Completion of the proof.\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the family \\(\\mathcal L_{k}\\) constructed above\n\n* consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines (Lemma\u202f6);\n* covers every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) (Lemma\u202f4);\n* contains exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Lemma\u202f5).\n\nHence every such \\(k\\) is attainable.\n\nConversely, any admissible configuration must contain exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, so the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative. Therefore the set of possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
